[Music]  far cry 5 can be a bit of a mess  it's AI is spotty its narrative is all  over the place and most of its plot is  told inches from the camera by  characters with unclear goals and  confusing motivations but still I adore  this game because this is one of  Ubisoft's best open worlds yet flashback  to one year ago games published by  Ubisoft were easily recognizable the UIs  were cluttered the screens were littered  with waypoints and the mini maps were  covered in a multitude of icons  collectibles and miscellaneous tasks but  lately that hasn't been the case with  last year's Assassin's Creed origins  Ubisoft Montreal shied away from its  usual formula it removed much of the  crowded UI and completely erased the  infamous mini-map origins emphasized  exploration not by giving you tasks to  complete but by showing you distant  structures on the horizon and plenty of  things to distract you along the way it  encouraged discovery without an array of  icons across the screen and now with far  cry 5 Ubisoft continues that shift from  artificial checklists to digital  adventure it marks a promising move in  the publishers evolution as well as the  futures of its open-world franchises  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  changes in far cry 5 come on both a  macro and micro level as was the case  with origins far cry 5 askew is a mini  map in favor of a compass this change  lifted from the elder scrolls may seem  minor but it makes the world feel more  organic it takes your eyes off the UI  and directs them toward your  surroundings by removing the whole  climate our to reveal more of the map  trope Far Cry 5 goes a step further and  encouraging you to just wander around  Ubisoft Montreal uses sound design to  its advantage as well many times  throughout my playthrough I would hear a  gunshot in the distance upon following  it I might come across the expected  firefight other times I might stumble  upon a hostage situation gone bad less  frequently still I would come across a  lone hunter skinning her prey each  scenario was as exciting as the next  precisely because they all started the  same way but ended very differently  Ubisoft has been calling many of its  games anecdote factories for a while now  the games have numerous connected  systems you can toy with allowing you to  manipulate your enemy's abilities or  environments the fun comes from watching  them interact or just as often seeing  them collide in Far Cry 5 these water  cooler moments are more frequent and  thrilling  [Music]  now I think far cry 5 is Ubisoft's best  anecdote factory specifically because of  the changes it makes to exploration by  placing more emphasis on self-driven  discovery like breath of the wild did  last year everything feels more natural  you learn about characters through  rumors at the local bar you find new  hunting grounds via signs along the road  you find an isolated base-jumping  location travel through its canyons and  learn exactly why the locals named it  Devil's drop this leads me to far craft  five subtle yet effective environmental  storytelling in this version of Montana  most of the locals have doomsday bunkers  with ample amounts of canned food and  bottled water I'm reminded of Fallout 3  while exploring these subterranean  hideouts scribbled notes suggest what  happened in the final days before the  homeowners were captured by the Eden's  gate cult  Ubisoft Montreal expertly makes use of  context throughout this world take this  side quest as another example in the  early hours of my playthrough I saw this  window peppered with bullet holes  shortly after I heard the source of  these markings a cultist firing his  weapon at the hut after killing him I  went to investigate the building finding  a note he had written telling passerby  he had crossed the pond to try to shoot  the lock off of the huts door not only  did this explain his antics but also  hinted that there might be something  valuable inside it turns out there was  on the surface this encounter may seem  basic but consider this  I could have stumbled upon it in five  different ways I could have read the  note I could have heard the cultist  gunshots I could have seen the locked  door from his vantage point I could have  heard a rumour about the weapons stash  from an NPC across the map or as was  actually the case I could have seen the  tell-tale markings around the window the  quest works no matter your entry point  that to me is a very well-designed  open-world quest  this is in no way the only mission  designed to fit seamlessly into far cry  fives Montana but it is the only one I  want to dissect here because most of the  fun comes from finding these moments  yourself and watching them all unfold  none of this mentions your AI companions  or guns for hire parking back to Far Cry  2 these allies lending even more flavor  to your personal stories you develop  your own connections to these people as  you spend time with them my friend Luke  Smith may be dumb as hell take Exhibit A  but he's been with me through thick and  thin he's that friend I can't help hang  out with despite how often he gets me in  trouble  you and me may have both played Far Cry  5 but you might never have met Luke  Smith and you probably never will  finally we come to Far Cry fives main  narrative as I said before I'm  conflicted too much of it is exposition  spoken through religious diatribes  straight to your face it's also a  mishmash of about 15 different themes  none of which are given enough time to  pay off or even develop in some cases  but I do respect the main storyline for  how you come across it as with the rest  of Far Cry fives world these plot points  aren't waypoints on a mini map or boxes  to be checked off a list they occur  naturally you're captured somewhere in  the wilderness probably as you're  pursuing some other goal and taken to  the necessary story mission  I'm sure this annoys a lot of people who  would just rather ignore the main story  but I like it one of the biggest  problems in open-world games is how to  tell an overarching narrative when many  if not most players will be focused on  finding their own anecdotes far cry 5s  plot may not be perfect but as far as  how Ubisoft weaves it into the greater  world it's on the right track this is my  biggest overarching sentiment for the  game in general it's a promising shift  for a great series it's focused on  discovery and organic exploration make  me excited for farcry future and the  future of Ubisoft open worlds in general  the publishers internal Studios have  been experimenting with this sandbox  formula for years introducing new  features and design philosophies as they  go they keep the ones that work and they  discard those that don't the animal  commands in Far Cry 4 became the  centerpiece of  our Cry primal naval warfare introduced  an assassin's creed 3 became a  full-fledged game in Black Flag and now  the self-driven exploration of  Assassin's Creed origins has become for  me the defining fact our a far cry 5 and  I'm excited to see what comes next

